From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shaurizard <contact@nyoom.engineering>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 20:45:48 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 33/34] system: install title update packages and apply the
 installed title update

A title update comes as an STFS package of content type 0x000B0000
(kInstaller), like the console's TU_* files. ReXApp's content_root
installs only marketplace packages, ContentManager::InstallContent files
every package as marketplace content of the running title, and nothing
applies an installed update: UserModule::LoadFromFile finds an XEX patch
only beside the XEX or in an update data folder of extracted files.

Port Xenia Canary's model. Like Canary's InstallContentPackage,
InstallContent takes the title and content type from the package header,
so a title update lands in <xuid 0>/<title>/000B0000/ where
ListContent(kInstaller) finds it. ReXApp installs DLC and title update
packages from content_root before the executable loads (the header names
each package's title, so no title ID is needed yet). When the module has
no sibling patch and no update: folder is mounted, LoadFromFile opens the
title update installed for the module's title as UPDATE: and looks for
the patch there, as Canary's KernelState::FindTitleUpdate and
LoadTitleUpdate do; the mount stays, since titles read their update data
files from update:. An update data folder keeps precedence. Canary's
apply_title_update cvar (default true) turns all of it off, for a title
recompiled from the base image.

OpenContent read the license mask from the header path of the running
title, which the executable defines; it now uses the content's own title,
which is the same for every caller so far and is known before the
executable is set.

Co-authored-by: factory-droid[bot] <138933559+factory-droid[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>
